  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Acute onset diarrhoea and vomiting for 3 days.\n- Symptoms: Watery then very loose diarrhoea; frequency at least 4 times today (lost count overall). Profuse vomiting (cannot keep fluids down, last vomited ~2 hours before call). Subjective high fever/feeling \"literally boiling\" (no thermometer reading). Periumbilical abdominal pain (centered around the navel), intermittent, associated with urgent need to defecate. Onset 3 days ago, progressive, not improved with oral rehydration solution; severity moderate–severe (unable to work outside home today).\n- Aggravating/alleviating: Eating/drinking provokes vomiting; small sips of ORS recommended but patient vomiting everything. No clear alleviating factors reported.\n- Past medical history: No chronic medical problems reported; occasional menstrual cramps. No prior similar episodes.\n- Medications / Allergies: Regular vitamin D. Occasionally ibuprofen for menstrual cramps. No drug allergies stated.\n- Family / social history: Lives with mother and brother; brother has had similar symptoms for a few days and reports school outbreak—suggests household/institutional exposure. Does not smoke; drinks alcohol occasionally (a pint sometimes). Works as a graphics designer; working from home today.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: No objective vitals recorded. Patient reports passing urine about an hour before call (subjective). Abdomen tender around the umbilical area on palpation (patient reports she \"can't touch my navel area\"). No rash. No blood seen in stool or urine.\n- Labs / imaging: None available; clinician recommended stool sample and hydration assessment. No tests performed yet.",
